fic_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\n# Paper strategy: response ceilings and targeted investment\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.\n**Provisional title:** Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards.\n\n## Scientific center\n\n**Question.** Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\n\nThis is a standalone conditional empirical study, not an optimizer breakthrough, a training experiment, or a search report. Organize the six-task and subject-level panels as complementary evidence blocks under one question. Their certificate strength differs; neither is evidence of architecture change or domain transfer. The contribution is the precisely defined investment comparison and its measured limits, not a claim that familiar enumeration or greedy selection is new.\n\n## Framing decision and rejected alternatives\n\nNo edit-context file, previous manuscript, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an assumed inherited improvement narrative; there is no actual prior manuscript to reject.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an optimizer-improvement story | Reject: the frozen selection is the reference baseline, not a successful new optimizer. |\n| Broaden to a shared novel certificate family | Reject: no component ablations substantiate the abandoned proposal mechanisms, and large-panel median/trimmed costs are not exact. |\n| Recenter on response-dependent investment | Choose: final ordinary decision records support direct rule-by-ceiling comparisons, including exact paired contrasts and explicit unattainability. |\n\nDirect inspection confirms that the staged selected and baseline source files are identical. Their small-panel enumeration, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses determine what can be claimed. The auxiliary projections and BBH records provide additional scope checks, not a reason to broaden the thesis to a new method family. The venue changes presentation, not the scientific conclusion.\n\n## Private grounding and qualification audit\n\nThis section is a provenance memo, not manuscript prose. Use these registered source identifiers in downstream claim mapping:\n\n- `final.qualification.status = completed`, `final.qualification.outcome = qualified`, and `final.qualification.correct = true`. These come from `paper_data.json.final_evaluation`, read independently of selection metadata. Qualification does not establish novelty, population generalization, or every support minimum.\n- `study.selection_kind = baseline`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`; `study.attempts.scored = 3`; `study.attempts.unscored = 0`. Search evidence and final measurements have distinct scopes. No search alternative has an established qualifying improvement. Missing performance evidence would not be a measured loss.\n- `final.evidence.episodes` contains three sampling seeds, ordinary and matched-control cases, primary and auxiliary decisions, and policy-comparison intervals. Its `candidate_diagnostics_trust` remains `unverified`. The evidence-file SHA-256 agrees with the manifest. Direct evaluator inspection shows witness verification and exhaustive small-panel reference computation; that does not license unrelated candidate diagnostics. The field `exact_audit_pass` alone is not proof of full-panel exactness.\n- Preserve the final qualification assessment through writing, review, audit, and revision. If authoritative later evidence changes it to a failure, disclose that failure explicitly and retain its limitations. Failed search alternatives have no established causal diagnosis: do not infer performance-only, validity, or adaptivity failure from a boolean outcome.\n\nThe cross-rule cohort check was a read-only summary of existing records, not a new experiment. Restrict to ordinary primary Open LLM decisions and join on seed and target slot. Intersect targets attainable under both mean and median at both half and full headroom. The resulting fixed cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. The following rounded values are audit checks derived from `final.evidence.episodes`, not hard-coded plotting inputs:\n\n| Sampling seed | Median minus mean support fraction, half headroom | Same contrast, full headroom |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1009 | -0.129630 | +0.138889 |\n| 1013 | -0.127451 | +0.127451 |\n| 1019 | -0.068627 | +0.156863 |\n\nAll feasible primary six-task costs in this contrast have exact support status and correspond to the frozen exhaustive procedure. The reversal is a descriptive within-snapshot result selected during framing, not a preregistered significance finding. Larger-panel HELM witness-cost reversals are not minimum-cost reversals when certification intervals overlap.\n\n## Scope and method groups\n\nDefine scores in `[0,1]`, a target model, a fixed rival pool, a task panel of size `m`, and selected support `S`. Only selected coordinates change, by `x_j -> x_j + alpha(1-x_j)`, with recorded `alpha` values `0.25`, `0.5`, and `1.0`. Unique first means target aggregate exceeds the largest rival aggregate by more than `1e-10`. Investment is `|S|`, with `|S|/m` used for normalized display. Total normalized score lift is secondary, not dollars, training examples, or compute.\n\nInclude only these method groups:\n\n1. **Frozen support-minimization procedure — supporting.** Exhaust at most six coordinates, sort headroom for larger means, and construct marginal-score-greedy supports for larger median/trimmed panels. Explain the all-coordinate infeasibility test and exact versus lower–upper outcomes.\n2. **Equal-task arithmetic mean — aggregation baseline.** Retain throughout; the headroom solution is exact.\n3. **Conventional median — supporting condition.** Average the two middle scores on even panels; do not substitute a different median convention.\n4. **Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean — supporting condition.** Remove `floor(m/5)` scores from each tail.\n5. **No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy policies — selection baselines.** Keep matched recorded comparisons, including ties. They are not component ablations.\n\nDo not require discarded rank-window or clipped-envelope branches in the main narrative. No evaluated Borda condition, equilibrium model, measured training response, architecture intervention, or unrun ablation belongs in this paper. State equal task weights, no spillover, no score regression outside the support, fixed rivals, and uncertain mapping from score uplift to actual training investment.\n\n## Results structure and uncertainty\n\nLead with the exact fixed-cohort mean–median contrast. Follow it with all-target attainability and support/certificate scope for both primary families. Maintain one definition of **target attainment rate**: the fraction of ordinary primary target decisions with a verified strict-first support and a supported claim. Do not mix controls into that rate.\n\nUse a compact main comparator table at half headroom for both families and all three rules, retaining ties with greedy and comparisons against random and no intervention. The complete rule/ceiling/seed grid and all recorded comparator intervals belong after references. A meaningful exact-selection advantage in one small-panel condition does not make the frozen reference a newly invented optimizer.\n\nThe main body must distinguish:\n\n- Exact six-task and mean minimum costs from larger median/trimmed feasible upper bounds and verified lower bounds.\n- Unattainable cases from unresolved support intervals. Never draw the `m+1` infeasibility sentinel as an actual intervention cost.\n- Successful-target support from a penalized effort summary: `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction` assigns one to nonattainment. Likewise, `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` measures distance to a verified lower bound, not necessarily true optimizer error.\n- Paired comparisons on identical targets from cross-ceiling summaries with different attainable cohorts.\n- Three sampling seeds from independent training replicates. Public pools and some targets/ancestry may overlap.\n\nExisting intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271, conditional on fixed snapshots and rival pools. They compare the selected procedure with random or greedy policies, not aggregation rules with each other. Do not reuse those intervals for the central reversal, invent p-values, or call the certification intervals statistical confidence intervals. Show all seed summaries and describe the cross-rule comparison as descriptive.\n\nKeep matched constant-ceiling, zero-response, permutation, and replay checks in a concise scope discussion; replay has statistical weight zero. Passing them establishes only the tested properties. Report auxiliary six-coordinate projections and revision-matched BBH separately, never pooled into primary scores or described as independent transfer replication.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` declares a **5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height** in a single-column ICLR layout. Thus `full_width` means the one text column, not a two-column spread. Half-width placement would provide only about 2.75 inches and is unsuitable for the planned multi-panel comparisons. Use ordinary full-text-width figures, not a two-column layout workaround.\n\n| Figure | Concrete design and source | Readability budget and footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Investment overview | `generate-concept-figure`; fixed scores/rivals, target-only headroom equation, three aggregation branches, exact/infeasible/bounded support outcomes. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, interpreted with inspected source/contract. No numerical toy result. | One shallow diagram, at most six visual groups and three aggregator branches; approximately 5.5 by 1.8 inches; full width on page 1 or 2; at least 9-point labels. |\n| 2. Exact rule contrast | `matplotlib`; two side-by-side signed dot plots at half and full headroom, each showing the per-seed median-minus-mean exact support fraction on the same fixed cohort. Source: ordinary primary Open LLM slices of `final.evidence.episodes`. | Two panels, two per row, three seed categories, one contrast series; approximately 5.5 by 2.1 inches; full width; same signed y scale and zero line; caption gives cohort counts. No invented error bars. |\n| 3. Attainability and support scope | `matplotlib`; 2-by-2 composite, rows HELM/Open LLM; left all-target attainment at three ceilings; right paired support bounds at half/full headroom. Source: ordinary primary `final.evidence.episodes`. | Four panels, two per row, at most three categories and three rule series per panel; approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches; full width, common legend and readable scales; at least 9-point labels. |\n\nFor Figure 3 right panels, intersect attainable targets across all three rules within each seed and ceiling, state cohort sizes, and average the within-seed lower and feasible upper fractions with declared equal seed weights. Unlike Figure 2, these cohorts may differ across ceilings. Exact costs collapse to points; other intervals are certification ranges. Show an empty cohort as not estimable, not as a cost or an imputed value. Do not use these changing-cohort panels alone to establish the central reversal.\n\nAll figures are direct condition comparisons; no optimization trajectories, milestone views, or campaign timelines. Use tables for scope, status counts, and policy differences rather than another dense dashboard. Quantitative figures must export vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI from source; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve editable overview source and prompt provenance. If only raster is available, regenerate at least 2400 pixels wide for full-width placement, or 1200 pixels for a later justified narrow placement; never upscale. Only the overview uses the concept-figure workflow. This stage does not generate figures or invoke that workflow.\n\n## Main-body budget and venue contract\n\nTarget ICLR 2026, anonymized, with about **8.5 substantive body pages excluding references**. Figure area is included in the following allocation:\n\n| Scientific use of space | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Abstract, introduction, precise contribution and scope | 1.25 |\n| Safely grounded related work and modeling context | 0.75 |\n| Bounded-response objective and assumptions | 1.00 |\n| Support computation and certificate scope | 1.00 |\n| Public-panel protocol, pairing and uncertainty | 0.75 |\n| Exact contrast, attainment, support bounds and comparators, including figures | 2.75 |\n| Limitations and conclusion | 1.00 |\n\nAn eight-page body counts only if its last page is at least 75 percent full. Expand useful model explanation, certificate reasoning, comparison clarity, and limitation analysis before adding visual or textual filler. Do not import the NeurIPS template or checklist obligation from a source-data manifest in place of the selected ICLR venue requirements. Use the provided ICLR style, with no author names, affiliations, identifying links, acknowledgements, or funding statement. Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, optionally after references, describing actual assistance only.\n\nSuggested headings: **Introduction; Related Work and Scope; Bounded-Response Investment; Computing Support and Certifying Limits; Public Score Panels and Comparison Protocol; Response-Dependent Aggregation Costs; Attainability and Certificate Scope; Limitations and Conclusion.**\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\nPurpose: add grounded mathematical, uncertainty, sensitivity, and reproduction depth without concealing evidence essential to the thesis. No new experiment or guessed later ranking is required. Route separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses here without retroactively modifying the strategy.\n\n| Planned subsection | Available grounded content | Registered source IDs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Support objective, aggregation conventions, and certificates | Full objective, strict margin, median/trim definitions, headroom argument, enumeration, infeasibility test, conservative-bound and feasible-witness reasoning, tied to inspected frozen source and `task/evaluate_final.py`. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Complete rule-by-response and comparator tables | All ordinary primary counts, support/status/bounds, zero/random/greedy comparisons and recorded conditional cluster intervals, including null effects. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.feasible_rate`; `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction`; `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` |\n| Task granularity and auxiliary sensitivity | Separate six-task projections and BBH records using evaluator routing, revision reconciliation, and distinct-run caveat; no unrun causal granularity experiment. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Matched controls and evaluation scope | Constant/zero-response/permutation/replay checks and their narrow interpretations; completed final qualification and retained evidence-trust caveat. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.control_pass_rate`; `final.qualification.status`; `final.qualification.outcome`; `final.qualification.correct` |\n| Frozen selection and unsuccessful alternatives | Brief nonchronological disclosure of baseline selection, zero search-score change, three scored and zero unscored attempts; unknown failure causes and search/final separation. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.investigation_outcome`; `study.search_score_delta`; `study.attempts.total`; `study.attempts.scored`; `study.attempts.unscored` |\n| Public data provenance and reproduction map | Existing source manifests, hashes, normalization/alias grouping, filtering, strata/seeds, CPU dependencies and commands; planned script-to-table/figure map; license uncertainty and evaluation-setting differences. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| LLM Usage | Disclose documented ideation, code preparation, query planning, and framing assistance; add writing/figure use only if it actually occurs. Do not invent identities, human checks, or training. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.attempts.total`; `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nThe listed IDs anchor evaluated outcomes; direct method and provenance details must additionally cite their actual local artifacts in the later evidence map, rather than pretending an episode record itself contains the entire source manifest.\n\n## Reproducibility and reference handoff\n\nUse HELM **v1.13.0**, Open LLM contents revision `9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2`, and the distinct results revision `aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5`. The final manifest records acquisition on 2026-11-17 and extracted-record SHA-256 `d80abb1b13c6962e15f04d9794fc18fcf39e91cf7c79c679e233d3031f934398`. Preserve each source checksum from `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, and distinguish public repository sizes from the evaluated extracted pools. Existing manifest and raw records support normalization, filtering, alias grouping, split, and setting caveats; do not claim perfectly homogeneous evaluation conditions.\n\nBBH reconciliation matches model revisions but does not prove identical evaluation settings. In the Open LLM auxiliary routing, odd slots use BBH and even slots use the primary-family projection. Do not match auxiliary slots to primary model identities without reconstructing their actual identifiers. Data-specific redistribution permission remains unresolved: public accessibility, code licenses, and model licenses do not establish a score-data redistribution license.\n\nThe writer must create deterministic derived-data/plot/table scripts, record their exact source slices and commands, and provide the dependency versions already available in `task/requirements.txt`. Those manuscript reproduction scripts are not present merely because the evaluated solver exists. Regeneration from existing records is not a new empirical run; any genuinely new analysis must be labeled and grounded separately.\n\nThe bibliography currently provides metadata retrieval, not evidence of substantive reading, and many entries are tangential. Several relevant starter records are absent. Before literature-dependent prose, curate safely and actually read the permitted source; use only available, verified citation keys and do not fabricate positioning or pad related work. This is a writer-stage prerequisite, not a reason to invent a novelty claim now. No external page was accessed during strategy generation. Do not identify or search for the prohibited study, expand citation graphs, or use discussion/aggregation pages; stop and log any accidental exposure.\n\n## Language guardrails\n\n**Forbid headings:** Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Timeline; Experiment A; Experiment B; From Baseline to Winner; Optimization Breakthroughs; Successful Certificate Variants.\n\n**Forbid phrases:** “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “winning implementation,” “state of the art,” “universally most robust aggregation,” “median always requires more investment,” “measured training cost,” “causal contamination effect,” and “all full-panel solutions are optimal.”\n\nPrivate-only terminology exclusions for manuscript prose: `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`. Prefer target attainment rate, support fraction, strict first place, exact minimum support, certified infeasibility, and feasible support interval.\n\n## Completion boundary\n\nThis strategy supports a coherent conditional scientific paper, not a claim of conference acceptance or an already-complete reproduction package. Core limitations, unsuccessful search disclosure, all-target attainment, conditioning definitions, and exact-versus-bounded scope remain in the main paper. No manuscript, claim-mapping JSON, figure, or new research experiment is produced here, and final-paper validation is not run. Bibliographic substantive reading, plotting scripts, manuscript drafting, and later validation remain downstream work.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; sed -n '1,200p' paper_strategy.md; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; sed -n '1,200p' paper_strategy.md; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\n# Paper strategy: response ceilings and targeted investment\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.\n**Provisional title:** Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards.\n\n## Scientific center\n\n**Question.** Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\n\nThis is a standalone conditional empirical study, not an optimizer breakthrough, a training experiment, or a search report. Organize the six-task and subject-level panels as complementary evidence blocks under one question. Their certificate strength differs; neither is evidence of architecture change or domain transfer. The contribution is the precisely defined investment comparison and its measured limits, not a claim that familiar enumeration or greedy selection is new.\n\n## Framing decision and rejected alternatives\n\nNo edit-context file, previous manuscript, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an assumed inherited improvement narrative; there is no actual prior manuscript to reject.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an optimizer-improvement story | Reject: the frozen selection is the reference baseline, not a successful new optimizer. |\n| Broaden to a shared novel certificate family | Reject: no component ablations substantiate the abandoned proposal mechanisms, and large-panel median/trimmed costs are not exact. |\n| Recenter on response-dependent investment | Choose: final ordinary decision records support direct rule-by-ceiling comparisons, including exact paired contrasts and explicit unattainability. |\n\nDirect inspection confirms that the staged selected and baseline source files are identical. Their small-panel enumeration, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses determine what can be claimed. The auxiliary projections and BBH records provide additional scope checks, not a reason to broaden the thesis to a new method family. The venue changes presentation, not the scientific conclusion.\n\n## Private grounding and qualification audit\n\nThis section is a provenance memo, not manuscript prose. Use these registered source identifiers in downstream claim mapping:\n\n- `final.qualification.status = completed`, `final.qualification.outcome = qualified`, and `final.qualification.correct = true`. These come from `paper_data.json.final_evaluation`, read independently of selection metadata. Qualification does not establish novelty, population generalization, or every support minimum.\n- `study.selection_kind = baseline`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`; `study.attempts.scored = 3`; `study.attempts.unscored = 0`. Search evidence and final measurements have distinct scopes. No search alternative has an established qualifying improvement. Missing performance evidence would not be a measured loss.\n- `final.evidence.episodes` contains three sampling seeds, ordinary and matched-control cases, primary and auxiliary decisions, and policy-comparison intervals. Its `candidate_diagnostics_trust` remains `unverified`. The evidence-file SHA-256 agrees with the manifest. Direct evaluator inspection shows witness verification and exhaustive small-panel reference computation; that does not license unrelated candidate diagnostics. The field `exact_audit_pass` alone is not proof of full-panel exactness.\n- Preserve the final qualification assessment through writing, review, audit, and revision. If authoritative later evidence changes it to a failure, disclose that failure explicitly and retain its limitations. Failed search alternatives have no established causal diagnosis: do not infer performance-only, validity, or adaptivity failure from a boolean outcome.\n\nThe cross-rule cohort check was a read-only summary of existing records, not a new experiment. Restrict to ordinary primary Open LLM decisions and join on seed and target slot. Intersect targets attainable under both mean and median at both half and full headroom. The resulting fixed cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. The following rounded values are audit checks derived from `final.evidence.episodes`, not hard-coded plotting inputs:\n\n| Sampling seed | Median minus mean support fraction, half headroom | Same contrast, full headroom |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1009 | -0.129630 | +0.138889 |\n| 1013 | -0.127451 | +0.127451 |\n| 1019 | -0.068627 | +0.156863 |\n\nAll feasible primary six-task costs in this contrast have exact support status and correspond to the frozen exhaustive procedure. The reversal is a descriptive within-snapshot result selected during framing, not a preregistered significance finding. Larger-panel HELM witness-cost reversals are not minimum-cost reversals when certification intervals overlap.\n\n## Scope and method groups\n\nDefine scores in `[0,1]`, a target model, a fixed rival pool, a task panel of size `m`, and selected support `S`. Only selected coordinates change, by `x_j -> x_j + alpha(1-x_j)`, with recorded `alpha` values `0.25`, `0.5`, and `1.0`. Unique first means target aggregate exceeds the largest rival aggregate by more than `1e-10`. Investment is `|S|`, with `|S|/m` used for normalized display. Total normalized score lift is secondary, not dollars, training examples, or compute.\n\nInclude only these method groups:\n\n1. **Frozen support-minimization procedure — supporting.** Exhaust at most six coordinates, sort headroom for larger means, and construct marginal-score-greedy supports for larger median/trimmed panels. Explain the all-coordinate infeasibility test and exact versus lower–upper outcomes.\n2. **Equal-task arithmetic mean — aggregation baseline.** Retain throughout; the headroom solution is exact.\n3. **Conventional median — supporting condition.** Average the two middle scores on even panels; do not substitute a different median convention.\n4. **Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean — supporting condition.** Remove `floor(m/5)` scores from each tail.\n5. **No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy policies — selection baselines.** Keep matched recorded comparisons, including ties. They are not component ablations.\n\nDo not require discarded rank-window or clipped-envelope branches in the main narrative. No evaluated Borda condition, equilibrium model, measured training response, architecture intervention, or unrun ablation belongs in this paper. State equal task weights, no spillover, no score regression outside the support, fixed rivals, and uncertain mapping from score uplift to actual training investment.\n\n## Results structure and uncertainty\n\nLead with the exact fixed-cohort mean–median contrast. Follow it with all-target attainability and support/certificate scope for both primary families. Maintain one definition of **target attainment rate**: the fraction of ordinary primary target decisions with a verified strict-first support and a supported claim. Do not mix controls into that rate.\n\nUse a compact main comparator table at half headroom for both families and all three rules, retaining ties with greedy and comparisons against random and no intervention. The complete rule/ceiling/seed grid and all recorded comparator intervals belong after references. A meaningful exact-selection advantage in one small-panel condition does not make the frozen reference a newly invented optimizer.\n\nThe main body must distinguish:\n\n- Exact six-task and mean minimum costs from larger median/trimmed feasible upper bounds and verified lower bounds.\n- Unattainable cases from unresolved support intervals. Never draw the `m+1` infeasibility sentinel as an actual intervention cost.\n- Successful-target support from a penalized effort summary: `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction` assigns one to nonattainment. Likewise, `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` measures distance to a verified lower bound, not necessarily true optimizer error.\n- Paired comparisons on identical targets from cross-ceiling summaries with different attainable cohorts.\n- Three sampling seeds from independent training replicates. Public pools and some targets/ancestry may overlap.\n\nExisting intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271, conditional on fixed snapshots and rival pools. They compare the selected procedure with random or greedy policies, not aggregation rules with each other. Do not reuse those intervals for the central reversal, invent p-values, or call the certification intervals statistical confidence intervals. Show all seed summaries and describe the cross-rule comparison as descriptive.\n\nKeep matched constant-ceiling, zero-response, permutation, and replay checks in a concise scope discussion; replay has statistical weight zero. Passing them establishes only the tested properties. Report auxiliary six-coordinate projections and revision-matched BBH separately, never pooled into primary scores or described as independent transfer replication.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` declares a **5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height** in a single-column ICLR layout. Thus `full_width` means the one text column, not a two-column spread. Half-width placement would provide only about 2.75 inches and is unsuitable for the planned multi-panel comparisons. Use ordinary full-text-width figures, not a two-column layout workaround.\n\n| Figure | Concrete design and source | Readability budget and footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Investment overview | `generate-concept-figure`; fixed scores/rivals, target-only headroom equation, three aggregation branches, exact/infeasible/bounded support outcomes. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, interpreted with inspected source/contract. No numerical toy result. | One shallow diagram, at most six visual groups and three aggregator branches; approximately 5.5 by 1.8 inches; full width on page 1 or 2; at least 9-point labels. |\n| 2. Exact rule contrast | `matplotlib`; two side-by-side signed dot plots at half and full headroom, each showing the per-seed median-minus-mean exact support fraction on the same fixed cohort. Source: ordinary primary Open LLM slices of `final.evidence.episodes`. | Two panels, two per row, three seed categories, one contrast series; approximately 5.5 by 2.1 inches; full width; same signed y scale and zero line; caption gives cohort counts. No invented error bars. |\n| 3. Attainability and support scope | `matplotlib`; 2-by-2 composite, rows HELM/Open LLM; left all-target attainment at three ceilings; right paired support bounds at half/full headroom. Source: ordinary primary `final.evidence.episodes`. | Four panels, two per row, at most three categories and three rule series per panel; approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches; full width, common legend and readable scales; at least 9-point labels. |\n\nFor Figure 3 right panels, intersect attainable targets across all three rules within each seed and ceiling, state cohort sizes, and average the within-seed lower and feasible upper fractions with declared equal seed weights. Unlike Figure 2, these cohorts may differ across ceilings. Exact costs collapse to points; other intervals are certification ranges. Show an empty cohort as not estimable, not as a cost or an imputed value. Do not use these changing-cohort panels alone to establish the central reversal.\n\nAll figures are direct condition comparisons; no optimization trajectories, milestone views, or campaign timelines. Use tables for scope, status counts, and policy differences rather than another dense dashboard. Quantitative figures must export vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI from source; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve editable overview source and prompt provenance. If only raster is available, regenerate at least 2400 pixels wide for full-width placement, or 1200 pixels for a later justified narrow placement; never upscale. Only the overview uses the concept-figure workflow. This stage does not generate figures or invoke that workflow.\n\n## Main-body budget and venue contract\n\nTarget ICLR 2026, anonymized, with about **8.5 substantive body pages excluding references**. Figure area is included in the following allocation:\n\n| Scientific use of space | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Abstract, introduction, precise contribution and scope | 1.25 |\n| Safely grounded related work and modeling context | 0.75 |\n| Bounded-response objective and assumptions | 1.00 |\n| Support computation and certificate scope | 1.00 |\n| Public-panel protocol, pairing and uncertainty | 0.75 |\n| Exact contrast, attainment, support bounds and comparators, including figures | 2.75 |\n| Limitations and conclusion | 1.00 |\n\nAn eight-page body counts only if its last page is at least 75 percent full. Expand useful model explanation, certificate reasoning, comparison clarity, and limitation analysis before adding visual or textual filler. Do not import the NeurIPS template or checklist obligation from a source-data manifest in place of the selected ICLR venue requirements. Use the provided ICLR style, with no author names, affiliations, identifying links, acknowledgements, or funding statement. Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, optionally after references, describing actual assistance only.\n\nSuggested headings: **Introduction; Related Work and Scope; Bounded-Response Investment; Computing Support and Certifying Limits; Public Score Panels and Comparison Protocol; Response-Dependent Aggregation Costs; Attainability and Certificate Scope; Limitations and Conclusion.**\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\nPurpose: add grounded mathematical, uncertainty, sensitivity, and reproduction depth without concealing evidence essential to the thesis. No new experiment or guessed later ranking is required. Route separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses here without retroactively modifying the strategy.\n\n| Planned subsection | Available grounded content | Registered source IDs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Support objective, aggregation conventions, and certificates | Full objective, strict margin, median/trim definitions, headroom argument, enumeration, infeasibility test, conservative-bound and feasible-witness reasoning, tied to inspected frozen source and `task/evaluate_final.py`. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Complete rule-by-response and comparator tables | All ordinary primary counts, support/status/bounds, zero/random/greedy comparisons and recorded conditional cluster intervals, including null effects. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.feasible_rate`; `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction`; `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` |\n| Task granularity and auxiliary sensitivity | Separate six-task projections and BBH records using evaluator routing, revision reconciliation, and distinct-run caveat; no unrun causal granularity experiment. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Matched controls and evaluation scope | Constant/zero-response/permutation/replay checks and their narrow interpretations; completed final qualification and retained evidence-trust caveat. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.control_pass_rate`; `final.qualification.status`; `final.qualification.outcome`; `final.qualification.correct` |\n| Frozen selection and unsuccessful alternatives | Brief nonchronological disclosure of baseline selection, zero search-score change, three scored and zero unscored attempts; unknown failure causes and search/final separation. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.investigation_outcome`; `study.search_score_delta`; `study.attempts.total`; `study.attempts.scored`; `study.attempts.unscored` |\n| Public data provenance and reproduction map | Existing source manifests, hashes, normalization/alias grouping, filtering, strata/seeds, CPU dependencies and commands; planned script-to-table/figure map; license uncertainty and evaluation-setting differences. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| LLM Usage | Disclose documented ideation, code preparation, query planning, and framing assistance; add writing/figure use only if it actually occurs. Do not invent identities, human checks, or training. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.attempts.total`; `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nThe listed IDs anchor evaluated outcomes; direct method and provenance details must additionally cite their actual local artifacts in the later evidence map, rather than pretending an episode record itself contains the entire source manifest.\n\n## Reproducibility and reference handoff\n\nUse HELM **v1.13.0**, Open LLM contents revision `9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2`, and the distinct results revision `aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5`. The final manifest records acquisition on 2026-11-17 and extracted-record SHA-256 `d80abb1b13c6962e15f04d9794fc18fcf39e91cf7c79c679e233d3031f934398`. Preserve each source checksum from `task/resources/final/manifest.json`, and distinguish public repository sizes from the evaluated extracted pools. Existing manifest and raw records support normalization, filtering, alias grouping, split, and setting caveats; do not claim perfectly homogeneous evaluation conditions.\n\nBBH reconciliation matches model revisions but does not prove identical evaluation settings. In the Open LLM auxiliary routing, odd slots use BBH and even slots use the primary-family projection. Do not match auxiliary slots to primary model identities without reconstructing their actual identifiers. Data-specific redistribution permission remains unresolved: public accessibility, code licenses, and model licenses do not establish a score-data redistribution license.\n\nThe writer must create deterministic derived-data/plot/table scripts, record their exact source slices and commands, and provide the dependency versions already available in `task/requirements.txt`. Those manuscript reproduction scripts are not present merely because the evaluated solver exists. Regeneration from existing records is not a new empirical run; any genuinely new analysis must be labeled and grounded separately.\n\nThe bibliography currently provides metadata retrieval, not evidence of substantive reading, and many entries are tangential. Several relevant starter records are absent. Before literature-dependent prose, curate safely and actually read the permitted source; use only available, verified citation keys and do not fabricate positioning or pad related work. This is a writer-stage prerequisite, not a reason to invent a novelty claim now. No external page was accessed during strategy generation. Do not identify or search for the prohibited study, expand citation graphs, or use discussion/aggregation pages; stop and log any accidental exposure.\n\n## Language guardrails\n\n**Forbid headings:** Search Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Timeline; Experiment A; Experiment B; From Baseline to Winner; Optimization Breakthroughs; Successful Certificate Variants.\n\n**Forbid phrases:** “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “winning implementation,” “state of the art,” “universally most robust aggregation,” “median always requires more investment,” “measured training cost,” “causal contamination effect,” and “all full-panel solutions are optimal.”\n\nPrivate-only terminology exclusions for manuscript prose: `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `combined_score`, `rubric`, `worker_01`, and `worker_02`. Prefer target attainment rate, support fraction, strict first place, exact minimum support, certified infeasibility, and feasible support interval.\n\n## Completion boundary\n\nThis strategy supports a coherent conditional scientific paper, not a claim of conference acceptance or an already-complete reproduction package. Core limitations, unsuccessful search disclosure, all-target attainment, conditioning definitions, and exact-versus-bounded scope remain in the main paper. No manuscript, claim-mapping JSON, figure, or new research experiment is produced here, and final-paper validation is not run. Bibliographic substantive reading, plotting scripts, manuscript drafting, and later validation remain downstream work.\n@Article{López-Pintado2009OnTC,\n author = {S. López-Pintado and J. Romo},\n journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n pages = {718 - 734},\n title = {On the Concept of Depth for Functional Data},\n volume = {104},\n year = {2009}\n}\n\n@Article{Olwell2001ReliabilityEA,\n author = {D. Olwell},\n booktitle = {Technometrics},\n journal = {Technometrics},\n pages = {104 - 105},\n title = {Reliability Engineering and Risk Analysis},\n volume = {43},\n year = {2001}\n}\n\n@Article{Rychlik2004OptimalBO,\n author = {T. Rychlik},\n journal = {Statistics},\n pages = {391-412},\n title = {Optimal bounds on l-statistics based on samples drawn with replacement from finite populations},\n volume = {38},\n year = {2004}\n}\n\n@Article{Papadatos2004BoundsOE,\n author = {N. Papadatos and T. Rychlik},\n journal = {Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference},\n pages = {317-336},\n title = {Bounds on expectations of L-statistics from without replacement samples},\n volume = {124},\n year = {2004}\n}\n\n@Article{Zuo2021NonasymptoticAA,\n author = {Y. Zuo},\n booktitle = {Statistical Papers},\n journal = {Statistical Papers},\n pages = {1465-1481},\n title = {Non-asymptotic analysis and inference for an outlyingness induced winsorized mean},\n volume = {64},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Ramsay2023DifferentiallyPP,\n author = {Kelly Ramsay and Dylan Spicker},\n booktitle = {Electronic Journal of Statistics},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Differentially private projection-depth-based medians},\n volume = {abs/2312.07792},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Nason2013NewCO,\n author = {G. Nason},\n title = {New Class of Location Measures, the Guard Estimator and Connections to Multiscale Variance Stabilization},\n year = {2013}\n}\n\n@Article{Geist2014AutomatedSF,\n author = {Christian Geist and U. Endriss},\n booktitle = {Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research},\n journal = {J. Artif. Intell. Res.},\n pages = {143-174},\n title = {Automated Search for Impossibility Theorems in Social Choice Theory: Ranking Sets of Objects},\n volume = {40},\n year = {2014}\n}\n\n@Article{Xu2024OpenToMAC,\n author = {Hainiu Xu and Run-Cong Zhao and Li-Xing Zhu and Jinhua Du and Yu-Lan He},\n booktitle = {Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics},\n pages = {8593-8623},\n title = {OpenToM: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Evaluating Theory-of-Mind Reasoning Capabilities of Large Language Models},\n year = {2024}\n}\n\n@Article{Konieczny2005PropositionalBB,\n author = {S. Konieczny and Ramón Pino Pérez},\n booktitle = {European Journal of Operational Research},\n journal = {Eur. J. Oper. Res.},\n pages = {785-802},\n title = {Propositional belief base merging or how to merge beliefs/goals coming from several sources and some links with social choice theory},\n volume = {160},\n year = {2005}\n}\n\n@Article{Mishra2023AIAA,\n author = {A. Mishra},\n booktitle = {Social Science Research Network},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {AI Alignment and Social Choice: Fundamental Limitations and Policy Implications},\n volume = {abs/2310.16048},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Chopra2006SocialCT,\n author = {Samir Chopra and A. Ghose and T. Meyer},\n booktitle = {Information Fusion},\n journal = {Inf. Fusion},\n pages = {61-79},\n title = {Social choice theory, belief merging, and strategy-proofness},\n volume = {7},\n year = {2006}\n}\n\n@Article{Subochev2018RankingJU,\n author = {A. Subochev and F. Aleskerov and V. Pislyakov},\n booktitle = {J. Informetrics},\n journal = {J. Informetrics},\n pages = {416-429},\n title = {Ranking journals using social choice theory methods: A novel approach in bibliometrics},\n volume = {12},\n year = {2018}\n}\n\n@Article{Fan2025SoMiToMEM,\n author = {Xian-Zhe Fan and Xu-Hui Zhou and Chuan-Yang Jin and Kolby Nottingham and Hao Zhu and Maarten Sap},\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {SoMi-ToM: Evaluating Multi-Perspective Theory of Mind in Embodied Social Interactions},\n volume = {abs/2506.23046},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Article{Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU,\n author = {Marc Lanctot and Kate Larson and Yoram Bachrach and Luke Marris and Zun Li and Avishkar Bhoopchand and Thomas W. Anthony and B. Tanner and Anna Koop},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory},\n volume = {abs/2312.03121},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Ciná2016ProvingCT,\n author = {G. Ciná and Ulle Endriss},\n booktitle = {Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems},\n journal = {Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems},\n pages = {963 - 989},\n title = {Proving classical theorems of social choice theory in modal logic},\n volume = {30},\n year = {2016}\n}\n\n@Article{Flanigan2023SmoothedAO,\n author = {Bailey Flanigan and Daniel Halpern and Alexandros Psomas},\n booktitle = {Workshop on Internet and Network Economics},\n pages = {290-309},\n title = {Smoothed Analysis of Social Choice Revisited},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Nebel2024ExtensiveMI,\n author = {Jacob M. Nebel},\n booktitle = {Theoretical Economics},\n journal = {Theoretical Economics},\n title = {Extensive measurement in social choice},\n year = {2024}\n}\n\n@Article{Ge2025OptimizedDI,\n author = {Luise Ge and G. Kehne and Yevgeniy Vorobeychik},\n booktitle = {AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},\n pages = {16930-16937},\n title = {Optimized Distortion in Linear Social Choice},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Keivanpour2014ADM,\n author = {Samira Keivanpour and H. Haleh and Hamed Shakouri Ganjavi},\n title = {A Decision Model for Selecting the Optimum Oil Production Profile Using Multi Criteria Decision Making and Social Choice Theory},\n year = {2014}\n}\n\n@Book{Blair2025GeneratingFC,\n author = {Carter Blair and Kate Larson},\n booktitle = {Adaptive Agents and Multi-Agent Systems},\n journal = {Proc. of the 25th International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems},\n title = {Generating Fair Consensus Statements with Social Choice on Token-Level MDPs},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Thakur2019MAJORITYSC,\n author = {Ashutosh Thakur and Stanford Gsb and A. Roth and Fuhito Kojima and S. Callander},\n title = {MAJORITY STABILITY: COMBINING SOCIAL CHOICE AND MATCHING THEORY TO UNDERSTAND INSTITUTIONAL STABILITY},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Article{Y2025TeoriDI,\n author = {Angye Mareta Y and Fachri Adnan and Afriva Khaidir},\n booktitle = {JOURNAL OF ADMINISTRATIVE AND SOCIAL SCIENCE},\n journal = {JOURNAL OF ADMINISTRATIVE AND SOCIAL SCIENCE},\n title = {Teori Dan Implementasi Pengambilan Keputusan Dan Kaitannya Dengan Teori Rational Choice Dalam Administrasi Publik},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Conference{Cai2016AMO,\n author = {W. Cai and Xiaodong Fu and Li Liu and Li-jun Liu and Qingsong Huang},\n booktitle = {Chinese Control and Decision Conference},\n journal = {2016 Chinese Control and Decision Conference (CCDC)},\n pages = {2126-2131},\n title = {A method of competition result evaluation based on social choice theory},\n year = {2016}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Kong2005OccupationCA,\n author = {H. Kong},\n title = {Occupation, Class, and Social Networks in Urban China*},\n year = {2005}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; sed -n '1,125p' paper_strategy.md; ls .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; ls .codex/skills/write-paper/scripts; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex; sed -n '1,125p' paper_strategy.md; ls .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts; ls .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts; ls .codex/skills/write-paper/scripts; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\n\\title{Grounded Experimental Summary}\n\\iclrfinalcopy\n\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nReplace with a concise grounded abstract summarizing setup, core result, and takeaway.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nReplace with grounded motivation, problem framing, and contributions.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\nSummarize relevant prior work using citation keys present in references.bib (for example, \\citep{templateReference2026}).\n\n\\section{Method}\nDescribe data, setup, and method details needed for reproducibility.\n\n\\section{Results}\nReport grounded quantitative and qualitative findings. Include figures and tables when useful.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nState key findings, limitations, and next steps supported by evidence.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n% REQUIRED: Replace this scaffold with substantive grounded content.\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Details}\nReplace with grounded extended methods, reproducibility details, additional analyses, or supporting tables and figures.\n\n\\end{document}\n# Paper strategy: response ceilings and targeted investment\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.\n**Provisional title:** Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards.\n\n## Scientific center\n\n**Question.** Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\n\nThis is a standalone conditional empirical study, not an optimizer breakthrough, a training experiment, or a search report. Organize the six-task and subject-level panels as complementary evidence blocks under one question. Their certificate strength differs; neither is evidence of architecture change or domain transfer. The contribution is the precisely defined investment comparison and its measured limits, not a claim that familiar enumeration or greedy selection is new.\n\n## Framing decision and rejected alternatives\n\nNo edit-context file, previous manuscript, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an assumed inherited improvement narrative; there is no actual prior manuscript to reject.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an optimizer-improvement story | Reject: the frozen selection is the reference baseline, not a successful new optimizer. |\n| Broaden to a shared novel certificate family | Reject: no component ablations substantiate the abandoned proposal mechanisms, and large-panel median/trimmed costs are not exact. |\n| Recenter on response-dependent investment | Choose: final ordinary decision records support direct rule-by-ceiling comparisons, including exact paired contrasts and explicit unattainability. |\n\nDirect inspection confirms that the staged selected and baseline source files are identical. Their small-panel enumeration, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses determine what can be claimed. The auxiliary projections and BBH records provide additional scope checks, not a reason to broaden the thesis to a new method family. The venue changes presentation, not the scientific conclusion.\n\n## Private grounding and qualification audit\n\nThis section is a provenance memo, not manuscript prose. Use these registered source identifiers in downstream claim mapping:\n\n- `final.qualification.status = completed`, `final.qualification.outcome = qualified`, and `final.qualification.correct = true`. These come from `paper_data.json.final_evaluation`, read independently of selection metadata. Qualification does not establish novelty, population generalization, or every support minimum.\n- `study.selection_kind = baseline`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`; `study.attempts.scored = 3`; `study.attempts.unscored = 0`. Search evidence and final measurements have distinct scopes. No search alternative has an established qualifying improvement. Missing performance evidence would not be a measured loss.\n- `final.evidence.episodes` contains three sampling seeds, ordinary and matched-control cases, primary and auxiliary decisions, and policy-comparison intervals. Its `candidate_diagnostics_trust` remains `unverified`. The evidence-file SHA-256 agrees with the manifest. Direct evaluator inspection shows witness verification and exhaustive small-panel reference computation; that does not license unrelated candidate diagnostics. The field `exact_audit_pass` alone is not proof of full-panel exactness.\n- Preserve the final qualification assessment through writing, review, audit, and revision. If authoritative later evidence changes it to a failure, disclose that failure explicitly and retain its limitations. Failed search alternatives have no established causal diagnosis: do not infer performance-only, validity, or adaptivity failure from a boolean outcome.\n\nThe cross-rule cohort check was a read-only summary of existing records, not a new experiment. Restrict to ordinary primary Open LLM decisions and join on seed and target slot. Intersect targets attainable under both mean and median at both half and full headroom. The resulting fixed cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. The following rounded values are audit checks derived from `final.evidence.episodes`, not hard-coded plotting inputs:\n\n| Sampling seed | Median minus mean support fraction, half headroom | Same contrast, full headroom |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1009 | -0.129630 | +0.138889 |\n| 1013 | -0.127451 | +0.127451 |\n| 1019 | -0.068627 | +0.156863 |\n\nAll feasible primary six-task costs in this contrast have exact support status and correspond to the frozen exhaustive procedure. The reversal is a descriptive within-snapshot result selected during framing, not a preregistered significance finding. Larger-panel HELM witness-cost reversals are not minimum-cost reversals when certification intervals overlap.\n\n## Scope and method groups\n\nDefine scores in `[0,1]`, a target model, a fixed rival pool, a task panel of size `m`, and selected support `S`. Only selected coordinates change, by `x_j -> x_j + alpha(1-x_j)`, with recorded `alpha` values `0.25`, `0.5`, and `1.0`. Unique first means target aggregate exceeds the largest rival aggregate by more than `1e-10`. Investment is `|S|`, with `|S|/m` used for normalized display. Total normalized score lift is secondary, not dollars, training examples, or compute.\n\nInclude only these method groups:\n\n1. **Frozen support-minimization procedure — supporting.** Exhaust at most six coordinates, sort headroom for larger means, and construct marginal-score-greedy supports for larger median/trimmed panels. Explain the all-coordinate infeasibility test and exact versus lower–upper outcomes.\n2. **Equal-task arithmetic mean — aggregation baseline.** Retain throughout; the headroom solution is exact.\n3. **Conventional median — supporting condition.** Average the two middle scores on even panels; do not substitute a different median convention.\n4. **Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean — supporting condition.** Remove `floor(m/5)` scores from each tail.\n5. **No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy policies — selection baselines.** Keep matched recorded comparisons, including ties. They are not component ablations.\n\nDo not require discarded rank-window or clipped-envelope branches in the main narrative. No evaluated Borda condition, equilibrium model, measured training response, architecture intervention, or unrun ablation belongs in this paper. State equal task weights, no spillover, no score regression outside the support, fixed rivals, and uncertain mapping from score uplift to actual training investment.\n\n## Results structure and uncertainty\n\nLead with the exact fixed-cohort mean–median contrast. Follow it with all-target attainability and support/certificate scope for both primary families. Maintain one definition of **target attainment rate**: the fraction of ordinary primary target decisions with a verified strict-first support and a supported claim. Do not mix controls into that rate.\n\nUse a compact main comparator table at half headroom for both families and all three rules, retaining ties with greedy and comparisons against random and no intervention. The complete rule/ceiling/seed grid and all recorded comparator intervals belong after references. A meaningful exact-selection advantage in one small-panel condition does not make the frozen reference a newly invented optimizer.\n\nThe main body must distinguish:\n\n- Exact six-task and mean minimum costs from larger median/trimmed feasible upper bounds and verified lower bounds.\n- Unattainable cases from unresolved support intervals. Never draw the `m+1` infeasibility sentinel as an actual intervention cost.\n- Successful-target support from a penalized effort summary: `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction` assigns one to nonattainment. Likewise, `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` measures distance to a verified lower bound, not necessarily true optimizer error.\n- Paired comparisons on identical targets from cross-ceiling summaries with different attainable cohorts.\n- Three sampling seeds from independent training replicates. Public pools and some targets/ancestry may overlap.\n\nExisting intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271, conditional on fixed snapshots and rival pools. They compare the selected procedure with random or greedy policies, not aggregation rules with each other. Do not reuse those intervals for the central reversal, invent p-values, or call the certification intervals statistical confidence intervals. Show all seed summaries and describe the cross-rule comparison as descriptive.\n\nKeep matched constant-ceiling, zero-response, permutation, and replay checks in a concise scope discussion; replay has statistical weight zero. Passing them establishes only the tested properties. Report auxiliary six-coordinate projections and revision-matched BBH separately, never pooled into primary scores or described as independent transfer replication.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` declares a **5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height** in a single-column ICLR layout. Thus `full_width` means the one text column, not a two-column spread. Half-width placement would provide only about 2.75 inches and is unsuitable for the planned multi-panel comparisons. Use ordinary full-text-width figures, not a two-column layout workaround.\n\n| Figure | Concrete design and source | Readability budget and footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Investment overview | `generate-concept-figure`; fixed scores/rivals, target-only headroom equation, three aggregation branches, exact/infeasible/bounded support outcomes. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, interpreted with inspected source/contract. No numerical toy result. | One shallow diagram, at most six visual groups and three aggregator branches; approximately 5.5 by 1.8 inches; full width on page 1 or 2; at least 9-point labels. |\n| 2. Exact rule contrast | `matplotlib`; two side-by-side signed dot plots at half and full headroom, each showing the per-seed median-minus-mean exact support fraction on the same fixed cohort. Source: ordinary primary Open LLM slices of `final.evidence.episodes`. | Two panels, two per row, three seed categories, one contrast series; approximately 5.5 by 2.1 inches; full width; same signed y scale and zero line; caption gives cohort counts. No invented error bars. |\n| 3. Attainability and support scope | `matplotlib`; 2-by-2 composite, rows HELM/Open LLM; left all-target attainment at three ceilings; right paired support bounds at half/full headroom. Source: ordinary primary `final.evidence.episodes`. | Four panels, two per row, at most three categories and three rule series per panel; approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches; full width, common legend and readable scales; at least 9-point labels. |\n\nFor Figure 3 right panels, intersect attainable targets across all three rules within each seed and ceiling, state cohort sizes, and average the within-seed lower and feasible upper fractions with declared equal seed weights. Unlike Figure 2, these cohorts may differ across ceilings. Exact costs collapse to points; other intervals are certification ranges. Show an empty cohort as not estimable, not as a cost or an imputed value. Do not use these changing-cohort panels alone to establish the central reversal.\n\nAll figures are direct condition comparisons; no optimization trajectories, milestone views, or campaign timelines. Use tables for scope, status counts, and policy differences rather than another dense dashboard. Quantitative figures must export vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI from source; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve editable overview source and prompt provenance. If only raster is available, regenerate at least 2400 pixels wide for full-width placement, or 1200 pixels for a later justified narrow placement; never upscale. Only the overview uses the concept-figure workflow. This stage does not generate figures or invoke that workflow.\n\n## Main-body budget and venue contract\n\nTarget ICLR 2026, anonymized, with about **8.5 substantive body pages excluding references**. Figure area is included in the following allocation:\n\n| Scientific use of space | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Abstract, introduction, precise contribution and scope | 1.25 |\n| Safely grounded related work and modeling context | 0.75 |\n| Bounded-response objective and assumptions | 1.00 |\n| Support computation and certificate scope | 1.00 |\n| Public-panel protocol, pairing and uncertainty | 0.75 |\n| Exact contrast, attainment, support bounds and comparators, including figures | 2.75 |\n| Limitations and conclusion | 1.00 |\n\nAn eight-page body counts only if its last page is at least 75 percent full. Expand useful model explanation, certificate reasoning, comparison clarity, and limitation analysis before adding visual or textual filler. Do not import the NeurIPS template or checklist obligation from a source-data manifest in place of the selected ICLR venue requirements. Use the provided ICLR style, with no author names, affiliations, identifying links, acknowledgements, or funding statement. Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, optionally after references, describing actual assistance only.\n\nSuggested headings: **Introduction; Related Work and Scope; Bounded-Response Investment; Computing Support and Certifying Limits; Public Score Panels and Comparison Protocol; Response-Dependent Aggregation Costs; Attainability and Certificate Scope; Limitations and Conclusion.**\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\nPurpose: add grounded mathematical, uncertainty, sensitivity, and reproduction depth without concealing evidence essential to the thesis. No new experiment or guessed later ranking is required. Route separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses here without retroactively modifying the strategy.\n\n| Planned subsection | Available grounded content | Registered source IDs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Support objective, aggregation conventions, and certificates | Full objective, strict margin, median/trim definitions, headroom argument, enumeration, infeasibility test, conservative-bound and feasible-witness reasoning, tied to inspected frozen source and `task/evaluate_final.py`. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Complete rule-by-response and comparator tables | All ordinary primary counts, support/status/bounds, zero/random/greedy comparisons and recorded conditional cluster intervals, including null effects. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.feasible_rate`; `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction`; `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` |\n| Task granularity and auxiliary sensitivity | Separate six-task projections and BBH records using evaluator routing, revision reconciliation, and distinct-run caveat; no unrun causal granularity experiment. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| Matched controls and evaluation scope | Constant/zero-response/permutation/replay checks and their narrow interpretations; completed final qualification and retained evidence-trust caveat. | `final.evidence.episodes`; `final.metric.control_pass_rate`; `final.qualification.status`; `final.qualification.outcome`; `final.qualification.correct` |\n| Frozen selection and unsuccessful alternatives | Brief nonchronological disclosure of baseline selection, zero search-score change, three scored and zero unscored attempts; unknown failure causes and search/final separation. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.investigation_outcome`; `study.search_score_delta`; `study.attempts.total`; `study.attempts.scored`; `study.attempts.unscored` |\n| Public data provenance and reproduction map | Existing source manifests, hashes, normalization/alias grouping, filtering, strata/seeds, CPU dependencies and commands; planned script-to-table/figure map; license uncertainty and evaluation-setting differences. | `final.evidence.episodes` |\n| LLM Usage | Disclose documented ideation, code preparation, query planning, and framing assistance; add writing/figure use only if it actually occurs. Do not invent identities, human checks, or training. | `study.selection_kind`; `study.attempts.total`; `final.evidence.episodes` |\n\nThe listed IDs anchor evaluated outcomes; direct method and provenance details must additionally cite their actual local artifacts in the later evidence map, rather than pretending an episode record itself contains the entire source manifest.\n\n## Reproducibility and reference handoff\nscientific_evidence.py\nscientific_evidence.sh\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nval